{% extends '_layout.jinja' %}
{% import 'components.jinja' as ui with context %}
{% import '_code.jinja' as code with context %}
{% from 'data/pages.jinja' import catalogue %}
{% block title %}{{ homepage.title }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{#- The stations a request passes, in the order the release runs them, named for
    the part of a vector display that does the same job. One list drives the
    journey section. -#}
{% set stations = [
  {'id': 'input', 'name': 'Input', 'line': 'Bytes off the socket',
   'detail': 'A Tokio TCP stream. The server reads first, under a 10-second deadline by default: a preamble if it expects one, otherwise the first byte.'},
  {'id': 'framing', 'name': 'Framing', 'line': 'Cut at the length prefix',
   'detail': 'A FrameCodec decodes frames. The default reads a 4-byte big-endian length prefix and refuses frames over 1,024 bytes.'},
  {'id': 'envelope', 'name': 'Envelope', 'line': 'Read the envelope',
   'detail': 'The serializer decodes an Envelope: a message ID, an optional correlation ID, and the payload bytes. Trailing bytes are a decoding error.'},
  {'id': 'reassembly', 'name': 'Reassembly', 'line': 'Fragments, rejoined', 'optional': true,
   'detail': 'With fragmentation enabled, fragments arrive inside envelopes and are rejoined before routing, bounded by size, by count, and by time.'},
  {'id': 'deflection', 'name': 'Deflection', 'line': 'Route by message ID',
   'detail': 'One handler per ID. An ID with no route is logged and dropped; registering an ID twice fails at setup.'},
  {'id': 'modulation', 'name': 'Modulation', 'line': 'Wrap the handler',
   'detail': 'The route’s middleware wraps its handler, so it sees the request on the way in and the reply on the way out.'},
  {'id': 'dwell', 'name': 'Dwell', 'line': 'Handle the message',
   'detail': 'The handler runs with the envelope it was sent. It returns nothing; the default reply echoes the payload.'},
  {'id': 'retrace', 'name': 'Retrace', 'line': 'Serialize, frame, send',
   'detail': 'The reply keeps the request’s correlation ID and leaves through optional fragmentation, the serializer, the codec’s wrapper, the before_send hook, and the encoder.'},
] %}
{% set capabilities = [
  {'icon': 'route', 'name': 'Routing', 'line': 'By message ID, one handler each', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/routing/'},
  {'icon': 'frame', 'name': 'Codecs', 'line': 'One trait for any wire format', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/codecs/'},
  {'icon': 'gates', 'name': 'Middleware', 'line': 'Wraps every route, in and out', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/middleware/'},
  {'icon': 'outbound', 'name': 'Client', 'line': 'The same codec and envelope, outbound', 'href': '/wireframe/guides/client/'},
] %}

<section class="wf-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="wf-shell wf-hero__grid">
    <div class="wf-hero__copy">
      <p class="wf-marque"><span>wireframe</span><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Common Signal</span></p>
      <h1 id="hero-title" class="wf-hero__title"><span class="wf-hero__kicker">A Rust router for binary protocols</span><img src="/wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-1400.webp" srcset="/wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-700.webp 700w, /wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(width < 640px) 90vw, 38rem" alt="wireframe" width="1400" height="357" fetchpriority="high"></h1>
      <p class="wf-hero__line">What we connect, we should understand.</p>
      <p class="wf-hero__deck">wireframe is an experimental Rust library for building servers and clients for frame-based binary protocols. Frame the bytes, route messages by ID, and keep protocol behaviour open to inspection.</p>
      {%- if wireframe_published %}
      <p class="wf-hero__status"><strong>{{ wireframe_version }} is an alpha for early adopters</strong>, released {{ wireframe_released }}. Interfaces may change before 1.0, so pin the exact version.</p>
      {%- else %}
      <p class="wf-hero__status"><strong>{{ wireframe_version }} is an alpha that is not yet published.</strong> It was targeted for {{ wireframe_released }} and is not on crates.io yet. Interfaces may change before 1.0, so pin the exact version.</p>
      {%- endif %}
      <div class="wf-hero__actions">{{ ui.button('Get started', '/wireframe/getting-started/') }}{{ ui.button('Read the guides', '/wireframe/guides/', kind='quiet') }}</div>
    </div>
    {{ ui.plate('27', eager=true, variant='hero') }}
  </div>
  <div class="wf-shell">
    <p class="wf-foundation"><strong>A common foundation for unfamiliar traffic.</strong> Every protocol brings its own rules. wireframe provides the framing and routing machinery, so an application can concentrate on the messages it handles.</p>
    <ul class="wf-capability-row">
      {%- for c in capabilities %}
      <li><a href="{{ c.href }}"><span class="wf-capability-row__icon">{{ ui.icon(c.icon) }}</span><span class="wf-label">{{ c.name }}</span><span>{{ c.line }}</span></a></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    <dl class="wf-readout" aria-label="At a glance">
      <div><dt>Release</dt><dd>{{ wireframe_version }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>{{ 'Alpha' if wireframe_published else 'Alpha, unpublished' }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>{{ 'crates.io' if wireframe_published else 'Target' }}</dt><dd>{{ wireframe_released }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Rust</dt><dd>{{ rust_minimum }} or newer</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Runtime</dt><dd>Tokio</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Licence</dt><dd>ISC</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-steps" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
  <div class="wf-shell">
    <h2 id="steps-title" class="sr-only">Three steps</h2>
    <ol class="wf-steps__grid">
      <li class="wf-panel">
        <h3 class="wf-panel__title"><span>1</span> Describe the messages</h3>
        {{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'messages', title='first_server.rs') }}
      </li>
      <li class="wf-panel">
        <h3 class="wf-panel__title"><span>2</span> Route and answer</h3>
        {{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'app', title='first_server.rs') }}
      </li>
      <li class="wf-panel wf-panel--reduction">
        <h3 class="wf-panel__title"><span>3</span> Complexity, reduced</h3>
        {{ ui.plate('28') }}
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="wf-steps__proof">Both panels are cut from <a href="/wireframe/getting-started/">the getting-started program</a>, compiled and run against {{ samples_verified_against }} on {{ samples_verified_on }}. A routed handler returns nothing; the program&rsquo;s middleware writes the <code>Pong</code>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-journey" aria-labelledby="journey-title">
  <div class="wf-shell wf-journey__grid">
    <div class="wf-journey__intro">
      <h2 id="journey-title">Frame. Route. Handle.</h2>
      <p><strong>Follow the message.</strong> A codec identifies frame boundaries. The routing layer selects a handler by message ID. Middleware surrounds the application work. The reply retraces the way it came, and the architecture guide explains each boundary.</p>
      <p class="wf-journey__note">Fragment reassembly is a transport step and optional. Message assembly, for protocols that spread one message across several frames, is a separate step that runs after it.</p>
      <a class="wf-arrow-link" href="/wireframe/architecture/">The stack, layer by layer {{ ui.icon('arrow-right') }}</a>
    </div>
    <ol class="wf-track">
      {%- for s in stations %}
      <li class="wf-track__stop{% if s.optional %} wf-track__stop--optional{% endif %}" id="station-{{ s.id }}">
        <span class="wf-track__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p class="wf-label">{{ s.name }}{% if s.optional %} <span class="wf-track__tag">Optional</span>{% endif %}</p>
        <h3>{{ s.line }}</h3>
        <p>{{ s.detail }}</p>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-capabilities" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title">
  <div class="wf-shell">
    <h2 id="capabilities-title">What it carries</h2>
    <div class="wf-bento">
      <a class="wf-bento__cell wf-bento__cell--codec" href="/wireframe/guides/codecs/">
        <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('frame') }}</span>
        <h3>Any wire format, one trait</h3>
        <p><code>FrameCodec</code> describes any wire format: how to cut frames, where the payload sits, and where a correlation ID lives.</p>
        <ul class="wf-bento__list">
          <li><strong>Hotline</strong><span>20-byte header, big-endian lengths, a transaction ID</span></li>
          <li><strong>MySQL</strong><span>3-byte little-endian length, 1-byte sequence number</span></li>
          <li><strong>RESP</strong><span>Typed lines: simple strings, integers, bulk strings</span></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="wf-frame-diagram" aria-hidden="true"><span class="wf-frame-diagram__len">00 00 00 0c</span><span class="wf-frame-diagram__body">12 payload bytes &hellip;</span></div>
        <p class="wf-bento__caption">The default frame: a 4-byte big-endian length, then the payload. The three codecs above ship as worked examples.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="wf-bento__cell" href="/wireframe/guides/middleware/">
        <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('gates') }}</span>
        <h3>Answer in middleware</h3>
        <p>A <code>Transform</code> wraps every route&rsquo;s handler. Setup and teardown hooks carry per-connection state.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="wf-bento__cell wf-bento__cell--raised" href="/wireframe/guides/streaming/">
        <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('pulses') }}</span>
        <h3>Push and streams, as parts</h3>
        <p>Priority push queues, a fair connection actor, and multi-frame responses. Tested, and not driven by <code>WireframeServer</code> in this alpha; main has since wired streaming responses.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="wf-bento__cell" href="/wireframe/guides/client/">
        <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('outbound') }}</span>
        <h3>One definition, both ends</h3>
        <p><code>WireframeClient</code> correlates replies, reads streams, and pools reusable sockets, each opened on first use.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="wf-bento__cell wf-bento__cell--raised" href="/wireframe/guides/testing/">
        <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('proof') }}</span>
        <h3>Test at the frame</h3>
        <p>Feed frames one byte at a time, fragment a payload, or pace a slow reader under paused time.</p>
      </a>
      <div class="wf-bento__cell wf-bento__cell--image">
        {{ ui.plate('15') }}
        <div class="wf-bento__copy">
          <span class="wf-bento__icon">{{ ui.icon('budget') }}</span>
          <h3><a href="/wireframe/guides/large-messages/">Large messages, bounded</a></h3>
          <p>Fragmentation, message assembly, and per-connection memory budgets that count fragment and assembly bytes together, slow reads at 80%, and close the connection past the cap.</p>
          <div class="wf-proposal">
            <p class="wf-proposal__label">Proposed · RFC 0007</p>
            <p><strong>Capacity is part of the design.</strong> A message leaving a queue has not necessarily left memory. The proposed budget contract accounts for retained data and admitted work by scope. <a href="{{ main_source_base }}/docs/rfcs/rfc-0007-budgets-scheduling-and-assembly.md">Review the proposal</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-world" aria-labelledby="world-title">
  {{ ui.plate('29', variant='wide') }}
  <div class="wf-shell wf-world__body">
    <h2 id="world-title">The scope</h2>
    <p><strong>The visible city.</strong> Roads carry signals towards destinations, and crossings make a chosen path legible. The plates draw a library as a city so that its routes can be seen, but the city is only the setting: the stages are the library&rsquo;s own. A codec cuts the byte stream into frames, the router sends each envelope to the one handler its message ID names, and middleware stands at that junction, in and out.</p>
    <ul class="wf-key-list" aria-label="How the plates use colour">
      <li><span class="wf-key" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Cobalt</strong>, solid: structure, roads, and frames</span></li>
      <li><span class="wf-key wf-key--signal" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Signal cyan</strong>, dashed: information and its labels</span></li>
      <li><span class="wf-key wf-key--flow" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Flow green</strong>, heavy: the route actually taken</span></li>
      <li><span class="wf-key wf-key--capacity" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Capacity magenta</strong>, dotted: budgets and allocation</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The city lights are illustration, not telemetry. Nothing on this site reports a live network.</p>
    <a class="wf-arrow-link" href="/wireframe/scope/">How this site is drawn {{ ui.icon('arrow-right') }}</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-band" aria-labelledby="mxd-title">
  <div class="wf-shell wf-band__grid">
    <div>
      <h2 id="mxd-title">Already carrying traffic</h2>
      <p>mxd, df12&rsquo;s Hotline server, depends on wireframe {{ mxd_wireframe_version }}. Its transport adapter frames and routes the Hotline protocol; its domain crate never imports wireframe. Hotline&rsquo;s framing ships with wireframe too, as a worked codec example.</p>
      <a class="wf-arrow-link" href="/mxd/">Read about mxd {{ ui.icon('arrow-right') }}</a>
    </div>
    <dl class="wf-band__facts">
      <div><dt>Protocol</dt><dd>Hotline, 1990s Mac file sharing and chat</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Transport</dt><dd><code>mxd-wireframe-server</code>, on wireframe {{ mxd_wireframe_version }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Boundary</dt><dd>Wireframe types stop at the transport adapter</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wf-install" aria-labelledby="install-title">
  <div class="wf-shell wf-install__grid">
    <div>
      <h2 id="install-title">Add it to Cargo.toml</h2>
      <p>The sample crate&rsquo;s dependencies, exactly as compiled, on Rust {{ rust_minimum }} or newer. <code>pool</code> and <code>testkit</code> are opt-in features; <code>metrics</code> and the bincode serializer are on by default.</p>
      {% call ui.callout('warning', 'Pin the exact version during the alpha') %}<p>One alpha may change what the last one offered, so the dependency names <code>={{ wireframe_version }}</code>. Moving from 0.3.0? <a href="{{ source_base }}/docs/v0-4-0-migration-guide.md">The migration guide</a> lists every change to act on, including those that still compile.</p>{% endcall %}
      <div class="wf-install__actions">{{ ui.button('Get started', '/wireframe/getting-started/') }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="wf-install__code">
      {{ code.code('Cargo.toml', 'deps', title='Cargo.toml', lexer='toml') }}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<div class="wf-tagline"><p class="wf-shell">What we connect, we should understand.</p></div>
{% endblock %}
